WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.730
Speaker 1: I want to start today with what
is happening in Cuba, which is both horrifying

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:08.309
and it's fascinating, it's politically relevant.

00:00:08.309 --> 00:00:13.990
And of course, we're all globally, as we'll
talk about in a moment, every country in some

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:21.410
way dealing with not just coronavirus generally,
but also this resurgence of the virus, despite

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:26.010
vaccinations starting to roll out because
of the very contagious Delta variant.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:29.420
All of this relates to what's going on in
Cuba.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:30.730
So let's start there.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:37.770
A very good article in the BBC explaining,
generally speaking, what's happening in Cuba

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:39.020
right now.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:43.570
If you've not been following it, there are
essentially three issues here that are leading

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:47.050
to massive protests in Cuba.

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:51.600
First is, as is written here, the coronavirus
crisis.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:57.460
The protests, the BBC writes, appear to be
the result of societal exhaustion stemming

00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:59.990
from acute economic and health crises.

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:04.050
The pandemic and economic measures taken by
the government have made life in Cuba increasingly

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:05.150
difficult.

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:10.260
The pen, the island which had kept the pandemic
under control in twenty twenty, has seen infections

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:13.050
explode in recent week weeks.

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:15.680
We'll look at those numbers momentarily.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:21.260
But records in terms of daily infections,
in terms of deaths, the health care system

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:24.490
on the brink of collapse, that is one piece
of it.

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:29.800
And the hashtag S.O.S Cuba has been trending
on Twitter.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:36.070
This is one of the you know, one of the areas
that we've seen open slightly in Cuba is that

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:37.650
of Internet access.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:44.470
And now that leading to the S.O.S Cuba hashtag,
which the government is not thrilled with.

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:46.060
And we will talk about that in a moment.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:48.820
Number two, the economic situation.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:56.729
Tourism is one of the main engines for the
Cuban economy, almost completely paralyzed

00:01:56.729 --> 00:01:59.430
as a result of the coronavirus pandemic.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:06.729
In addition, Cuba is seeing inflation, blackouts,
food shortages, as well as shortages of medicine

00:02:06.729 --> 00:02:07.740
and basic products.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:11.080
Those aren't new issues, but they have been
particularly worse lately.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:14.920
At the beginning of the year, the government
proposed a new package of economic reforms

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:18.760
that while increasing wages triggered a spike
in prices prices.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:26.030
Economists such as Pavel Vidal from the Pontificator
have Indiana University of Caleigh and Columbia

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:30.500
estimate prices could rise between five hundred
and nine percent in the next few months.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:36.420
Starting to look like my birth country of
Argentina from the standpoint of inflation.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.780
And then number three, Internet access.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:44.010
Before Sunday, the largest protest Cuba had
seen since the start of Castro's communist

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:47.849
revolution took place in August of 94.

00:02:47.849 --> 00:02:51.680
Many Cubans had no idea what happened because
of lack of Internet access.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.840
30 years on, the scenario is different.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:03:00.310
Under the presidency of Raul Castro, Cuba
has liberalized, taking some liberalizing

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:02.870
steps which increased Internet connectivity.

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:08.069
And as I mentioned earlier, as a result of
that, Cubans have been using social networks

00:03:08.069 --> 00:03:11.500
to express dissatisfaction with the government.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:15.720
Mainly young people now have access to Facebook,
Twitter and Instagram.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:21.850
And this platform was these platforms were
used to organize the protests.

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:26.599
So then we look at, well, what is the U.S.
perspective on this?

00:03:26.599 --> 00:03:33.099
Because the US has had crippling sanctions
on Cuba for a very long time, sanctions that

00:03:33.099 --> 00:03:41.440
while I am not a communist and I am against
the way that Cuba has operated with no open

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:47.440
elections for decades, despite promises made
during the 1950s and 60s, I'm completely against

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:48.440
that.

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:53.849
I also recognize the total hypocrisy of the
US saying, well, we can't tacitly endorse

00:03:53.849 --> 00:03:57.190
the way Cuba is run and so we're putting sanctions
on them.

00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:01.010
And yet we have the relationship we have with
Saudi Arabia and many other countries.

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:02.660
OK, so I've talked about that before.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:09.489
Joe Biden yesterday during a press event bringing
up the Cuban protests and saying the US stands

00:04:09.489 --> 00:04:10.810
with the people of Cuba.

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:12.040
Take a look at this.

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:20.009
Speaker 2: OK, folks, I want to start by recognizing
the remarkable protests that are taking place

00:04:20.009 --> 00:04:27.120
in Cuba, the Cuban people demanding their
freedom from an authoritarian regime.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:33.550
And I don't think we've seen anything like
this protest in a long, long time.

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:40.379
Of course, frankly, ever the United States
stands firmly with the people of Cuba as they

00:04:40.379 --> 00:04:46.940
assert their universal rights and become the
government of Cuba to refrain from violence

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:50.819
or attempts to silence the voice of the people
of Cuba.

00:04:50.819 --> 00:04:54.030
And we're also closely following the developments
in Haiti.

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:58.490
Speaker 1: So this is a complicated issue.

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:06.669
And anybody you know, you've got a lot of
very right wing Cubans in Florida, for example,

00:05:06.669 --> 00:05:12.960
who love Trump and who are saying we need
to continue crushing the Cuban government

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:16.960
and punishing we're just punishing the people
if we do that.

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:23.169
On the other hand, we also have to recognize
that part of why things are the way they are

00:05:23.169 --> 00:05:28.389
in Cuba is because of American policy towards
Cuba for decades.

00:05:28.389 --> 00:05:33.849
And so anybody who wants to sort of pigeonhole
me on this issue and say, well, bit, but so,

00:05:33.849 --> 00:05:35.969
David, are you for or against or what?

00:05:35.969 --> 00:05:41.839
You know, we have to recognize that on the
one hand, the U.S. has had a major role in

00:05:41.839 --> 00:05:47.219
keeping things worse in Cuba than they could
otherwise be.

00:05:47.219 --> 00:05:49.669
We should be easing the sanctions that Trump
put in.

00:05:49.669 --> 00:05:52.439
Remember that Barack Obama eased some sanctions.

00:05:52.439 --> 00:05:56.689
Donald Trump came in and reinstituted a lot
of those sanctions.

00:05:56.689 --> 00:06:00.370
And although Joe Biden is on the one hand
saying we're with the Cuban people, the very

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:05.240
sanctions that Joe Biden has not eased that
were put back in place by Donald Trump are

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:06.869
part of why things are so bad.

00:06:06.869 --> 00:06:13.110
At the same time, I have been unquestionably
against the authoritarian regime in Cuba all

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:14.219
of this time.

00:06:14.219 --> 00:06:20.399
But we must recognize that the U.S. is friendly
with many authoritarian regimes.

00:06:20.399 --> 00:06:24.699
And so singling out Cuba has been a hypocrisy
for decades.

00:06:24.699 --> 00:06:33.249
Obviously, my main concern is the Cuban people,
and these are massive and significant protests

00:06:33.249 --> 00:06:35.199
because of the Internet access.

00:06:35.199 --> 00:06:40.020
This may be that we're seeing the repression
from the police state of Cuba and we're getting

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:48.089
reports of, you know, officers on every corner
and everything you can imagine would go on

00:06:48.089 --> 00:06:50.139
is going on at the same time.

00:06:50.139 --> 00:06:56.410
We can't ignore the U.S. role in Cuba being
at least partially the way that it is now.

00:06:56.410 --> 00:07:00.659
We'll see over the next 48 hours the direction
that the government response to the protests

00:07:00.659 --> 00:07:51.240
is going 

00:07:51.240 --> 00:08:00.949
to take.

